# Marrow Journal: signed-in architecture

Status: proposed implementation plan, 2026-10-18. Nothing is provisioned or implemented by this document. [brief.md](brief.md) is the product authority and wins over this plan and repository defaults.

## Decisions and assumptions

Marrow is a personal journal. Each authenticated person owns one account and all entries in it. There are no workspaces, organizations, memberships, invitations, roles for collaborators, shared journals, or public entry links. This follows the brief's permanent exclusion of collaboration; a hidden personal workspace would add an unnecessary ownership layer. Keep the auth provider's individual-account mode and leave organization features disabled.

The brief decides managed authentication with social sign-in and email, one subscription per account, and the five signed-in surfaces. The following are implementation decisions made while the owner is away:

| Area | Decision / assumption |
| --- | --- |
| Providers | Clerk for identity, Convex for application data and live queries, Stripe for subscriptions. Keep each behind modules named by role: `auth`, `journal`, `billing`. |
| Browser app | A React/TypeScript Vite SPA in `apps/app`, with a persistent editor shell and client-side routing. A future public site is a separate `apps/site` deployable with server or static rendering. |
| Sign-in methods | Google plus verified email codes, without passwords at launch. Additional social providers are not needed to satisfy the brief. |
| Daily journal | One entry per account per local calendar date. Plain text initially; no attachments, AI processing, or derived summaries. |
| Privacy model | Application-enforced owner access with managed infrastructure encryption in transit and at rest; this plan does **not** provide end-to-end encryption or a cryptographic guarantee against infrastructure operators. See the privacy boundary below. |
| Payment lapse | Preserve entries; allow reading, search, deletion, account management, and export. Require an active subscription for creating or editing entries. Prices, trials, grace periods, and retention promises remain undecided. |
| Hosts | Public site at the eventual apex and app at `app.<domain>` (or `web.<domain>` if the chosen apex uses `.app`). No domain has been chosen or purchased. |

These assumptions are reviewable decisions, not requests blocking this plan. Before launch, the owner should review especially the encryption model, daily-entry rule, and subscription-lapse behavior. Do not present undecided pricing or unimplemented privacy guarantees as product promises.

## Authentication and authenticated entry

Use one product route, `/auth`, for combined sign-in and sign-up through the managed integration. Verification and OAuth continuation belong to this route's provider handler, not separate product destinations. Use an embedded provider component supporting a combined flow; pin and verify its behavior against the installed SDK. Clerk documents its combined flow and email-code options in [sign-in-or-up guidance](https://clerk.com/docs/guides/development/custom-flows/authentication/sign-in-or-up). Enable enumeration protection and let the provider handle verification, recovery, credentials, rate limits, and session security.

Use custom Google OAuth credentials for development/test and production separately, with account selection enabled. Request identity scopes only, with no Google data access. Provider-managed linking may join verified identities; never merge journal accounts by matching a client-supplied email. Changing email or linking a sign-in method must preserve the stable provider subject and the application account. If a provider change yields a different subject, use an explicit verified migration rather than an automatic merge.

Every direct protected-route load follows this sequence:

1. Mount the route's empty shell and resolve the managed session. Signed-out visitors go to `/auth?returnTo=<encoded-relative-path>`.
2. Establish the backend's authenticated connection using the supported [Clerk–Convex integration](https://docs.convex.dev/auth/clerk). A browser session alone does not authorize backend queries.
3. Run an idempotent backend `ensureAccount` transaction using the verified issuer and subject. Check closure records first, then find or create the single application account. Initialize its timezone once from a validated browser suggestion; default to UTC if unavailable. Concurrent first loads must converge on the same account.
4. Start owner-scoped live queries only after bootstrap succeeds. Do not depend on a signup webhook, create an entry on page load, or require a refresh after first login.
5. Restore the validated intended route. With no destination, replace history with `/today`. An already signed-in visitor to `/auth` uses this same resolver in place.

Derive `returnTo` centrally from the interrupted app URL. Accept only supported relative app paths on the current origin; reject absolute URLs, protocol-relative paths, malformed encodings, auth loops, and control characters. Never put tokens, email addresses, journal text, or search text in it. The OAuth SDK owns its state/PKCE handling. Internal navigation uses relative links; cross-surface links use one stage-aware origin helper.

The backend validates the provider-issued token signature, issuer, expiry, and the integration's configured audience contract. Inspect a real development token during implementation rather than guessing claims. No trust in decoded-but-unverified claims or a browser-supplied account ID. Logout tears down subscriptions and clears journal data, unsaved editor buffers, and search state from memory before another account can render. Do the same on an identity switch or expired session; recovery must clearly explain any unsaved text loss.

## Ownership and authoritative data

Use the application account's internal ID as the ownership key. The auth subject identifies that account; an email address never does. Entry ownership is immutable. A different signed-in person cannot gain access by knowing a URL, paying for a subscription, or presenting another account ID.

| Record / fact | Authority and minimum shape |
| --- | --- |
| Login identity, verified email, connected providers, sessions | Clerk. Account stores only the stable issuer/subject reference needed to resolve identity; do not copy email or provider profiles without a product need. |
| Account and preferences | Convex `accounts`: internal ID, auth issuer/subject, IANA timezone, creation time, lifecycle state (`active` / `deleting`). The account itself is the personal ownership boundary. |
| Journal entry | Convex `entries`: internal ID, opaque URL `id`, immutable `ownerId`, `entryDate` (`YYYY-MM-DD`), plain-text `body`, creation/update timestamps, revision. |
| Subscription and payment | Stripe is authoritative. Store the account-to-customer reference and a reconciled last-known subscription projection for backend entitlement checks, never an independently editable `isPaid` flag. |
| Search representation and calendar summaries | Derived from entries in the same backend. They inherit owner scope and deletion; they are not independent journal copies. |
| Route rules, ID format, entitlement policy | Reviewed TypeScript in `packages/domain`. Provider SDK types and payloads remain inside backend adapters. |

Application indexes: unique logical lookup by `(authIssuer, authSubject)`, unique entry URL ID, and `(ownerId, entryDate)` for calendar reads and daily-entry lookup. Enforce uniqueness transactionally; do not assume an ordinary index provides a uniqueness constraint. Generate URL IDs with a cryptographic helper: 12 case-sensitive Base58 characters, no prefix, checked for collisions and retried. Database IDs and auth subjects do not appear in browser routes.

Derive today's date from the account's saved timezone. The backend computes the date rather than trusting a client clock. A timezone change affects future date resolution, not existing entry dates or identities. Opening an empty date shows an editor without persisting a blank entry; first save transactionally creates or resolves the day's entry. Calendar creation for a past date validates that date and uses the same transaction. Revision checks prevent a second tab silently overwriting newer text; keep the unsaved buffer and offer a conflict resolution when revisions differ.

One product authorization module resolves the live account and checks ownership for every journal capability: `readEntry`, `saveEntry`, `listCalendar`, `searchEntries`, `deleteEntry`, and `exportJournal`. This is an owner policy, not an extensible team role system. Queries begin with an owner index/filter; direct entry lookups check `ownerId` before returning any field. Unknown capabilities fail closed. Missing and foreign entries return the same product-owned unavailable response, without title, date, snippet, or existence information.

All transports call these capabilities, including internal tasks that act for an account. Jobs carry the owning account ID and recheck lifecycle state in each transaction; a successful check when scheduling is insufficient. No generic browser-facing database read or mutation may bypass this boundary. UI hiding and route guards improve the experience but grant no permissions. Entitlement checks are separate from owner authorization: a subscriber can only access their own journal, and a lapsed subscriber still owns theirs.

## Privacy, search, and deletion

The normal product exposes entry content only to its author. There is no staff journal viewer, impersonation workflow, share permission, public search index, third-party content analytics, or session replay on private screens. Keep bodies, search terms, snippets, auth tokens, and raw provider payloads out of logs, errors, traces, event metadata, and billing metadata. Use fixed private-page titles rather than entry-derived browser titles. Do not persist journal caches in localStorage, a service worker, or shared HTTP caches. Clear account-scoped client state on identity changes.

Managed hosting and database operators may technically access plaintext under this model. Restrict production administrative access, audit infrastructure access, and keep content out of support tools. Verify the actual provider encryption, retention, and access configuration before publishing security claims. If “only the author” must also exclude operators cryptographically, this assumption must change before storing real entries: use client-side encryption and redesign search, key recovery, and multi-device key management. Managed login alone does not supply that guarantee.

Search covers the author's entry body and date, not account/profile data. Use backend full-text indexing with `ownerId` as a mandatory equality filter on every query; [Convex search indexes support filter fields](https://docs.convex.dev/search/text-search). Include only eligible live entries and normalize search text through one helper. Calendar queries return dates and minimal state rather than every body. Search snippets return only after the same owner check. Document and test engine matching behavior when implementing search; do not promise semantic or exact-phrase search by default.

For privacy, search text is deliberately transient in memory and sent only in authenticated backend calls. `/search` is addressable, but reloading it clears the search phrase. This is an explicit exception to URL-persisted filters: journal searches can disclose the same sensitive information as entries. Do not put phrases in query strings, fragments, analytics, or `returnTo`.

Deleting an entry immediately excludes it from reads, calendar, and search, then removes its body and derived search state. No trash or historical-body retention is assumed. Account deletion requires recent provider reauthentication and explicit confirmation. Mark the account deleting, record a closure marker, and deny all ordinary capabilities before enqueueing bounded, resumable cleanup of entries, indexes, caches, billing references, and provider identity. Delete the account row last. Keep only required billing records at the billing provider, without entry text, and document backup expiration and any required retention before promising erasure timing.

Verify auth and billing webhook signatures before parsing trusted events; deduplicate by provider event ID. Auth identity deletion locates the account by stable issuer/subject and queues the same cleanup. Prevent old signed tokens from recreating deleted accounts with a minimal hashed-subject closure record. Product-initiated closure remains blocked while the provider identity can still mint credentials; a later identity-deletion event must not shorten that protection. Provider-originated deletion tombstones can expire after a verified upper bound on token validity plus a safety margin. Cleanup delivery and cross-provider failures retry idempotently without reopening access.

## URL contract

All routes below are relative to the app origin. No account or tenant prefix is needed: there is exactly one ownership scope selected by the verified identity. Do not include `/w/:workspaceId`, `/organizations`, usernames, email addresses, or journal titles. Copying a link allows the same author to reopen it on another signed-in device; it never gives a recipient access.

| Path | Meaning and behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `/` | Entry dispatcher; after auth/bootstrap, history-replace to `/today`. |
| `/auth` | Combined authentication, with optional validated `returnTo`. |
| `/today` | Dynamic editor for the current local date; existing content resolves by owner/date. This bookmark intentionally follows the day. |
| `/entries?month=2026-10` | Calendar collection. Without `month`, show the current account-local month. An explicitly selected month remains in the URL. |
| `/entries/:id` | One saved entry's stable, opaque URL, regardless of later date or display changes. Calendar and search results link here. |
| `/entries/new?date=2026-10-17` | Unsaved past-date editor. Reserve `new` in the route matcher; it cannot match the fixed-length entry-ID format. If the date already has an entry, replace with its canonical entry URL. First save also replaces with that URL. |
| `/search` | Owner-only search view; search phrase remains in memory as described above. Results link to entry IDs. |
| `/settings` | Account preferences, including timezone and identity management. |
| `/settings/billing` | The account's subscription and action to enter the managed billing portal. Portal return goes here. |
| `/settings/export` | Owner-authorized journal export request and delivery; never a public permanent download URL. |
| `/settings/delete-account` | Account deletion confirmation and reauthentication flow. |

`/today` is a relative-date shortcut, whereas `/entries/:id` permanently identifies an entry. Neither creates a second stored entry. When midnight changes the date, preserve any unsaved buffer and ask the writer to save or discard it before switching the editor; never silently retarget pending text. Auth and billing technical webhook endpoints live on the backend deployment under `/webhooks/auth` and `/webhooks/billing`; they are not browser pages and never accept a browser account ID as authority.

Central route builders parse and validate IDs, calendar months, and real calendar dates. Reject malformed values with product-owned errors; unknown paths show an app not-found screen. Use history pushes for navigation between entries and months, replaces for dispatch, auth completion, and creation-to-saved transitions. Editor text, cursors, unsaved buffers, and feedback stay outside URLs. Browser refresh and Back/Forward restore the addressed view. Configure SPA hosting rewrites so opening a deep link directly serves the app shell. Private routes are excluded from sitemaps and carry `noindex`; these are discovery controls, not authorization.

## Implementation boundaries and verification

Keep schema, ownership enforcement, jobs, and provider adapters in `packages/backend`; pure routes, date validation, ID generation, and policy in `packages/domain`. Apps import these packages, never the reverse. Subscribe through the authenticated reactive client so saves update today's view, calendar, search, and other tabs. Handle conflicts without discarding user-authored text. Keep runtime `.env.example` contracts next to each actual runtime; public browser keys and backend secrets are separate. Routes and product policy live in code, not environment variables.

Use isolated auth, database, OAuth, and test billing resources for development/staging versus production. Final domains, provider accounts, callback origins, webhook URLs/secrets, and exact SDK settings are deferred until provisioning establishes their real values. No production settings, paid resources, DNS, or external accounts are changed by this plan. Provisioning must verify real sign-in, first-load bootstrap, logout, webhook delivery, and billing reconciliation before it is called complete.

Implementation acceptance checks:

- Two independent accounts cannot read, edit, search, export, or delete one another's entries, even with valid foreign IDs or forged owner fields. Search and calendar reveal no foreign metadata.
- Google and email sign-in work for new and returning users, email changes preserve ownership, interrupted deep links return correctly, and hostile redirect values fail closed.
- Concurrent first loads yield one account; concurrent first saves yield one daily entry. Delayed or duplicated webhooks do not break either flow.
- Session expiry, logout, identity switching, and deletion stop protected live reads and clear the previous person's data. A stale valid token cannot bootstrap a closed account.
- Calendar/date parsing, timezone changes, midnight transitions, entry-ID stability, refresh, and browser history follow the URL contract. A direct deep link works from a cold browser.
- Search never places terms in URLs or logs; entry and account deletion remove active search results. Cleanup resumes after interruption and repeated events.
- Payment lapse and billing outages follow an explicit tested entitlement policy; payment status never changes content ownership or exposes content. Stripe portal access and customer lookup are bound server-side to the acting account.

This document is the deliverable for the planning task. Runtime claims remain unverified until implementation and provider setup.
